In the long run you'll do better starting with a basic
course.  Who has an easier time writing poetry: Someone
who learned the principles first or someone who started
writing lines to see how they work out?  Note the
difference in how much effort gets put into the initial
learning vs how much easier it is later.  That's called
the 'learning curve'.  I would rather point to to how
to move up the learning curve first.  Yes, you can
download the source, start beating on it, and progress
from there.  Taking that route, you are unlikely to end
up a top notch professional respected in the field.
Or you can start with principles, use those principles
as a structure to build on, have a good chance of ending
up a top notch professional respected in the field.

